Body:
Switching from `createMemo` to plain functions here can multiply work within a single render. In this component, `summary()` is read multiple times (read/search/list counts), and each call runs `contextToolSummary` (3 filters). Memoizing `pending`/`summary` (or at least `summary`) avoids repeated scans over `props.parts` during one reactive update.
```suggestion
  const pending = createMemo(
    () =>
      !!props.busy ||
      props.parts.some((part) => part.state.status === "pending" || part.state.status === "running"),
  )
  const summary = createMemo(() => contextToolSummary(props.parts))
```

`new URL(url)` will throw if `OPENCODE_SERVER_URL` is present but not a valid absolute URL (env vars can be user-controlled). To avoid reintroducing the same failure mode as the previous throwing getter, wrap parsing in a try/catch (or validate) and return `undefined` on invalid values (optionally log a warning).
```suggestion
        try {
          return new URL(url)
        } catch (error) {
          log.warn("invalid OPENCODE_SERVER_URL, ignoring value", { value: url, error })
          return undefined
        }
```

When both Windows arch metadata files exist, this merge unconditionally combines `files` while taking `version`/`releaseDate` from whichever one is chosen as `base`. If the arm64 and x64 `latest.yml` are ever generated with different `version` or `releaseDate` (e.g., one job used stale artifacts or reran), this will silently publish inconsistent updater metadata. Consider validating that both parsed `LatestYml` objects agree on `version` (and ideally `releaseDate`) before merging, and fail fast (or pick a deterministic precedence) if they differ; also consider de-duping `files` by `url`/`sha512` in case of overlaps.
```suggestion

  let files: FileEntry[] = [...(winArm64?.files ?? []), ...(winX64?.files ?? [])]

  if (winX64 && winArm64) {
    if (winX64.version !== winArm64.version) {
      throw new Error(
        `Mismatched Windows latest.yml versions: x64=${winX64.version}, arm64=${winArm64.version}`,
      )
    }
    if (winX64.releaseDate !== winArm64.releaseDate) {
      throw new Error(
        `Mismatched Windows latest.yml releaseDate: x64=${winX64.releaseDate}, arm64=${winArm64.releaseDate}`,
      )
    }

    const seen = new Set<string>()
    files = files.filter((f) => {
      const key = `${f.url}|${f.sha512}`
      if (seen.has(key)) return false
      seen.add(key)
      return true
    })
  }

  output["latest.yml"] = serialize({
    version: base.version,
    files,
```

`disk.clear()` skips a store entirely when `Store.load(name)` fails (`if (!store) return`). Since `getStore()` already anticipates `Store.load` can fail (and falls back to an in-memory store), this means "Clear cache" may not actually fix a corrupted on-disk store file. Consider adding a fallback that force-resets the underlying file when load fails (e.g., delete/overwrite the store file via a Tauri command or fs plugin) so clearing works even in the failure mode this feature is meant to address.
```suggestion
          if (store) {
            await store.clear().catch(() => undefined)
            await store.save().catch(() => undefined)
            return
          }

          // If loading the store failed (e.g., due to a corrupted file),
          // create a fresh store and save it to reset the underlying file.
          const freshStore = new Store(name)
          await freshStore.clear().catch(() => undefined)
          await freshStore.save().catch(() => undefined)
```

If `clearAllBrowsingData()` throws, `clearCache()` aborts before calling `reboot()`, leaving the user on the error page even though the disk store may already have been cleared. Consider ensuring the relaunch happens in a `finally` (or catching browsing-data errors and continuing) so the action reliably completes.
```suggestion
      try {
        await getCurrentWebview().clearAllBrowsingData()
      } catch {
        // Ignore errors when clearing browsing data; still proceed to reboot.
      }
```
